### the chance model of paper Section 7.6 (ssec:scanweight)
python            : 3.14.2
scanned primes    : ../data/all_primes_vreg.txt

the scanned set
  number of primes                   1611                   paper 1611
  smallest, largest                  5, 29989               paper 5, 29989
  lambda = sum 1/p                   0.880191               paper 0.880...

probability of a clean scan
  (H1) no exceptions                 1                      paper not printed
  (H2) chance, exp(-lambda)          0.4147                 paper 0.41
  (H3) c = 5, exp(-5 lambda)         0.0123                 paper not printed

likelihood ratios
  (H1):(H2) = exp(lambda)            2.411                  paper not printed
  (H1):(H3) at c = 5                 81.529                 paper not printed
  (H1):(H3) at c = 1                 2.411                  paper not printed
  (H3) at c = 1 is (H2), so the two ratios agree.

the extension to every split prime below 10^6
  number of split primes             39175                  paper not printed
  lambda                             1.026174               paper not printed
  clean probability exp(-lambda)     0.3584                 paper not printed
  (H1):(H2) = exp(lambda)            2.790                  paper not printed

the opposite outcome
  Poisson(lambda), P(X >= 5)         2.131e-03              paper not printed

exact Bernoulli model, for comparison with the Poisson approximation
  The paper states the Poisson approximation.  Under the independent
  Bernoulli model itself the clean probability is the product of
  1 - 1/p over the scanned primes.
  product of (1 - 1/p)               0.4023                 paper not printed
  Poisson exp(-lambda)               0.4147                 paper 0.41

